---

<CalculatorLayout
  title="Lean Body Mass Calculator"
  description="Calculate your Lean Body Mass (LBM) free using the validated Boer formula. Estimate your muscle vs fat mass weight instantly. 100% private."
  keyword="lean body mass calculator"
  canonicalPath="/calculator/lean-mass"
  formula="Boer clinical formula"
  lastModified={new Date('2026-07-02')}
  faqs={faqs}
  relatedCalculators={relatedCalculators}
  accuracyRating="±3–5%"
>

  <!-- ── SLOT: calculator ── -->
  <LeanMassCalculator slot="calculator" />

  <!-- ── SLOT: what-is ── -->
  <Fragment slot="what-is">
    <p>
      A <strong>lean body mass calculator</strong> is a tool designed to estimate your lean mass
      (also known as fat-free mass). Your lean body mass represents the total weight of all the non-fat
      components of your body, including skeletal muscles, bones, internal organs, skin, connective
      tissues, and body water. Knowing your LBM is essential for setting precise macronutrient targets
      and assessing body composition.
    </p>
    <p>
      In fitness and medical science, separating total weight into lean body mass and fat mass is critical.
      While fat mass serves as an energy storage reserve, lean body mass represents the metabolically active
      machinery of the body. A higher lean body mass elevates your <strong>resting metabolic rate (BMR)</strong>,
      meaning you burn more calories at rest, making weight management and long-term fat loss significantly
      more manageable.
    </p>
    <p>
      LBM is also the primary metric used in clinical pharmacology to determine safe, effective dosages for
      complex medications, such as anesthetics or chemotherapy agents. Because muscle clears medications
      differently than adipose tissue, calculating dosages based on lean mass prevents toxic drug levels in
      overweight patients and ensures efficacy in muscular individuals.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our calculator utilizes the <strong>Boer Formula</strong>. Developed in 1984, the Boer formula is
      one of the most widely cited and validated clinical models, using weight, height, and biological
      sex to estimate LBM with high precision.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- ── SLOT: how-to ── -->
  <Fragment slot="how-to">
    <p>
      The Boer equations are gender-specific, adjusting for differences in bone density and fat distribution
      patterns between men and women.
    </p>

    <div class="formula-block" role="math" aria-label="Boer lean body mass formulas">
      <p class="formula-label">The Boer Equations (Metric Units)</p>
      <code class="formula-code">Male Formula:
Lean Body Mass (kg) = 0.407 × Weight (kg) + 0.267 × Height (cm) − 19.2

Female Formula:
Lean Body Mass (kg) = 0.252 × Weight (kg) + 0.473 × Height (cm) − 48.3

Worked Example (Male: 180 cm tall, 80 kg weight):
LBM = 0.407 × 80 + 0.267 × 180 − 19.2
    = 32.56 + 48.06 − 19.2
    = 61.42 kg (Lean Mass)
Fat Mass = 80 kg − 61.42 kg = 18.58 kg
Body Fat % = (18.58 ÷ 80) × 100 = 23.2%</code>
      <p class="formula-source">
        Source: Boer P. (1984). "Relation between body mass and body fluids in clinical medicine."
        <em>American Journal of Physiology</em>, 247(2 Pt 1), F213-F218.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Step-by-Step Calculation</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Multiply your weight in kilograms by the gender coefficient</strong> (0.407 for men, 0.252 for women).</li>
      <li><strong>Multiply your height in centimeters by the height coefficient</strong> (0.267 for men, 0.473 for women).</li>
      <li>Add the two products together, then subtract the constant (19.2 for men, 48.3 for women) to find your LBM.</li>
      <li>Subtract your LBM from your total weight to calculate your total Fat Mass.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- ── SLOT: ranges ── -->
  <Fragment slot="ranges">
    <p>
      Unlike BMI, lean body mass does not have standardized health risk ranges. Instead, it is expressed
      as a percentage of your total weight (Lean Mass Ratio) to evaluate body composition.
    </p>

    <h3>Lean Mass Percentage Ranges</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Classification</th>
          <th>Men\'s Lean Mass Ratio</th>
          <th>Women\'s Lean Mass Ratio</th>
          <th>Body Composition Profile</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>High Muscle / Athlete</strong></td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">87% +</td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">80% +</td>
          <td>Very lean, highly defined musculature. Typical of competitive athletes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Fit / Healthy</strong></td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">83 – 86%</td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">76 – 79%</td>
          <td>Good muscle definition, low body fat. Healthy athletic profile.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Average</strong></td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">76 – 82%</td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">68 – 75%</td>
          <td>Standard composition. Moderate muscular profile, typical active adult.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Below Average</strong></td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">75% or lower</td>
          <td style="font-family:var(--font-mono)">67% or lower</td>
          <td>Higher body fat ratio, lower muscle density. Strength training recommended.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>LBM and Your Protein Targets</h3>
    <p>
      In sports science, daily protein targets are often calculated based on **Lean Body Mass** rather
      than total body weight. This is because fat tissue does not require protein for repair or synthesis.
      A standard recommendation is to consume <strong>2.0 to 2.3 grams of protein per kilogram of lean
      body mass</strong>. This method is highly effective for overweight or obese individuals, preventing
      unrealistically high protein targets that occur when using total weight.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

</CalculatorLayout>

